# Trivy IaC findings accepted for this project, each with the reason.
#
# WHY A BASELINE INSTEAD OF LEAVING THEM NOISY
# --------------------------------------------
# `trivy config` reports 21 findings here. Four of the HIGH/CRITICAL ones are
# intentional properties of the design, not defects. Left unsuppressed, every run
# prints the same 21 and the scan stops being read — the same failure mode as a
# gate that can never fail, arrived at from the opposite direction. Baselining the
# accepted ones lets the scan be a real gate: it fails on anything NEW.
#
# Adding a line here is a decision, not housekeeping. Each needs a reason that
# would survive someone asking "why is this safe?" — and anything whose answer is
# "it's probably fine" does not belong in this file.
#
# Reviewed: 2026-09-19 (P7.6). Re-review when the topology changes, and in P9.3
# when IRSA and real IAM boundaries land.

# ─── AWS-0104 (CRITICAL) — unrestricted egress to 0.0.0.0/0 ──────────────────
# The application's core function is calling third-party LLM APIs: Groq, OpenAI,
# Clerk JWKS, and Langfuse. Those are internet endpoints with rotating IP ranges
# behind CDNs, so an egress allowlist by CIDR is not maintainable and would fail
# closed at the worst time — mid-request, when a provider rotates an address.
#
# The mitigation is not egress CIDRs; it is that nothing in the cluster holds data
# worth exfiltrating in the clear (Secrets are KMS-envelope-encrypted per
# AWS-0039) and egress is from private subnets through a NAT gateway, so no
# workload is reachable inbound.
#
# REVISIT IF: the app ever handles regulated data, at which point egress goes
# through a proxy with an allowlist and this line is removed.
AWS-0104

# ─── AWS-0164 (HIGH) — subnet associates public IP address ───────────────────
# Flagged on aws_subnet.public, whose purpose is to be public: it carries the ALB
# and the NAT gateway. A subnet that cannot assign public IPs cannot host either.
#
# What matters is what is NOT in it: EKS nodes, RDS and ElastiCache are all in
# private subnets. The finding is the scanner reading a property without the
# intent, and the intent is legible from the resource name.
#
# REVISIT IF: any workload subnet starts setting map_public_ip_on_launch.
AWS-0164

# ─── AWS-0132 (HIGH) — S3 bucket not encrypted with a customer-managed key ───
# The buckets use SSE-S3 (AES-256), so data IS encrypted at rest. The finding is
# specifically that the key is AWS-managed rather than customer-managed.
#
# A CMK buys key-rotation control, per-key access policy, and an audit trail of
# key use. That is worth real money and operational burden when the bucket holds
# regulated data; these hold Terraform state (already access-blocked and
# versioned) and build artifacts. The Phase 1 NFRs put SOC 2 and HIPAA out of
# scope for v1, which is the condition that makes SSE-S3 sufficient.
#
# REVISIT IF: SOC 2 comes into scope — it is listed as a v1 non-goal precisely so
# this kind of decision has a written trigger rather than being re-litigated.
AWS-0132

# ─── AWS-0136 (HIGH) — SNS topic not encrypted with a customer-managed key ───
# The topic carries billing-threshold notifications: "spend crossed 75% of
# budget". It is encrypted with alias/aws/sns, so the objection is again
# AWS-managed vs customer-managed rather than plaintext.
#
# A CMK here would add key policy and rotation management to protect text that
# contains no credentials, no personal data, and nothing an attacker could use.
# Noted because the previous state (no encryption at all, AWS-0095) WAS worth
# fixing and was fixed in P7.6 — this is the residual stricter rule, not the
# original gap.
AWS-0136
